 ],
 "literal": "O gentle-natured one! O gentle-natured one! / Good modesty abandoned, free from blame, beautiful dark complexion, / with fortitude enduring, other than that, if spoken, / can words express, O gentle-natured one? / In the town where the lowly and the great live, / without a day's gap, of abundant water's flood, / like a huge tree on the firm bank, / in a faultless state, we shall embrace many times.",
 "translation": "O gentle friend! O gentle friend! Other than bearing this with quiet fortitude, if we try to speak of it, can words ever express our grief? In this town where both the small-minded and the noble live, like a huge tree standing on the firm bank of a river with an unceasing, abundant flood, let us embrace him many times in a faultless state.",
 "modern": "மென்மையான இயல்புடைய தோழியே! மென்மையான இயல்புடைய தோழியே! நல்ல நாணத்தை விடுத்து, பழி நீங்கிய எனது மாமை நிறத்தை மன வலிமையோடு தாங்கிக் கொள்வதைத் தவிர, வாய்திறந்து சொன்னால் வார்த்தைகளால் அதனை விவரிக்க முடியுமா? சிறியோரும் பெரியோரும் வாழ்கின்ற இந்த ஊரிலே, ஒரு நாளும் இடைவிடாது பெருகி ஓடும் நிறைந்த நீர்ப் பெருக்கினைக் கொண்ட ஆற்றின் வலிமையான கரையில் நிற்கும் பெரிய மரத்தைப் போல, குற்றமற்ற நிலையில் நாம் பலமுறை தழுவிக் கொள்வோம்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the Marutam (agricultural) landscape, where the heroine speaks to her close friend about her longing and the gossip of the town. The heroine expresses that her grief and desire cannot be fully put into words; instead, she must endure her situation with inner strength. She uses a vivid natural metaphor: a huge tree standing resiliently on the firm bank of a river that is constantly flooded with abundant water. The unceasing flood represents the relentless gossip of the townspeople, both small and great, while the firm bank represents her own modesty and fortitude. Just as the tree clings to the bank despite the rushing waters, she resolves to remain steadfast and embrace her lover many times in a faultless, virtuous state.",
